  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x² + z²)/2, drawn at 0.25 × loss, so x is the steep direction. Start at x = 1, z = 2. Each step: settings −= learning rate × (true slope + noise). Above a learning rate of 0.5, swings along x grow until the run diverges.",
    "Noise on each slope component is Gaussian with standard deviation 2·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, like sampling b of 64 points. It is exactly 0 at b = 64. With noise, a step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at their true step length in bowl units (scale 1). The orange arrow is the actual mini-batch step. The grey one is the step the full-data slope alone would give.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as diverged once the loss passes 12 or the ball leaves the drawn square |x|, |z| ≤ 2.4. Positions beyond the square are drawn at its edge, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. New run, keep last path continues the noise sequence, so its noise differs. Reset replays the sequence from the beginning. While running, the ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed."
  ]
}